1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,887
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,887 --> 00:00:07,384
This is chapter twenty-six: the Java memory model.

3
00:00:07,834 --> 00:00:11,675
This chapter explains why the rules in the last one work.

4
00:00:11,675 --> 00:00:19,695
Once you understand one relation, called happens-before, you can prove concurrent code correct instead of hoping it is.

5
00:00:20,595 --> 00:00:22,552
This program can print zero.

6
00:00:22,552 --> 00:00:26,257
Thread A sets value to forty-two, then ready to true.

7
00:00:26,257 --> 00:00:29,961
Thread B sees ready is true, yet value is still zero.

8
00:00:29,961 --> 00:00:33,246
The writes appear to happen in the wrong order.

9
00:00:33,696 --> 00:00:34,976
Nothing is broken.

10
00:00:34,976 --> 00:00:41,374
The compiler may reorder the two writes, because within thread A their order is invisible.

11
00:00:41,374 --> 00:00:44,928
The CPU may reorder them through its store buffer.

12
00:00:44,928 --> 00:00:48,056
And B's cache may update ready before value.

13
00:00:48,056 --> 00:00:48,767
All legal.

14
00:00:48,767 --> 00:00:50,971
Worse, it's platform-dependent.

15
00:00:50,971 --> 00:00:53,388
On x86 this almost never shows up.

16
00:00:53,388 --> 00:00:58,293
On ARM, including cloud instances and Apple Silicon laptops, it does.

17
00:00:59,193 --> 00:01:00,722
There's no global now.

18
00:01:00,722 --> 00:01:03,917
Each core has its own store buffer and caches.

19
00:01:03,917 --> 00:01:10,031
A write becomes visible to other cores eventually, and not necessarily in program order.

20
00:01:10,481 --> 00:01:13,727
The Java Memory Model doesn't describe hardware.

21
00:01:13,727 --> 00:01:19,139
It's an abstract contract that defines which values a read is allowed to return.

22
00:01:19,139 --> 00:01:27,526
The JVM then implements that contract on any hardware, inserting barriers wherever the hardware is weaker than the contract.

23
00:01:28,426 --> 00:01:31,062
Everything is built on one relation.

24
00:01:31,062 --> 00:01:34,869
If X happens-before Y, X's effects are visible to Y.

25
00:01:34,869 --> 00:01:40,799
Within a thread, program order gives it: setting value happens before the unlock.

26
00:01:41,249 --> 00:01:46,625
The monitor rule says an unlock happens before every later lock of the same monitor.

27
00:01:47,075 --> 00:01:49,614
Then program order again in thread B.

28
00:01:50,064 --> 00:01:52,274
And happens-before is transitive.

29
00:01:52,274 --> 00:01:58,235
Chain the rules into a path from a write to a read, and the read is guaranteed to see it.

30
00:01:58,685 --> 00:02:00,940
Here's the payoff, called DRF-SC.

31
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:09,960
A data race is two accesses to the same variable, at least one a write, from different threads, with no happens-before between them.

32
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:15,905
Eliminate every data race, and your program behaves as if statements ran one at a time.

33
00:02:15,905 --> 00:02:20,210
You don't need to reason about store buffers and caches at all.

34
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:24,546
The language rules: program order within a thread.

35
00:02:24,546 --> 00:02:27,845
Unlocking a monitor before any later lock of it.

36
00:02:27,845 --> 00:02:30,525
A volatile write before any later read.

37
00:02:30,525 --> 00:02:33,549
Starting a thread before everything it does.

38
00:02:33,549 --> 00:02:38,497
And everything a thread does before another thread's join on it returns.

39
00:02:38,947 --> 00:02:44,983
And java dot util dot concurrent documents its own edges, which is what makes it composable.

40
00:02:44,983 --> 00:02:49,707
Putting into a blocking queue happens before the take that retrieves it.

41
00:02:49,707 --> 00:02:55,218
Submitting a task happens before it runs, and completion happens before get returns.

42
00:02:55,218 --> 00:03:00,664
That's why handing an object through a queue is safe with no other synchronisation.

43
00:03:01,564 --> 00:03:04,820
Fix the publisher by making just ready volatile.

44
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:05,974
Here's the proof.

45
00:03:05,974 --> 00:03:08,687
One happens before two by program order.

46
00:03:08,687 --> 00:03:14,047
Two happens before three by the volatile rule, since three read what two wrote.

47
00:03:14,047 --> 00:03:16,964
Three happens before four by program order.

48
00:03:16,964 --> 00:03:21,916
So by transitivity, one happens before four, and four must see forty-two.

49
00:03:22,366 --> 00:03:25,010
Notice value didn't need to be volatile.

50
00:03:25,010 --> 00:03:28,844
The volatile write piggybacks every plain write before it.

51
00:03:28,844 --> 00:03:37,107
That's how nearly all correct concurrent code works: one synchronised access carrying the visibility of everything before it.

52
00:03:38,007 --> 00:03:43,886
An object is safely published when its reference and its state become visible together.

53
00:03:43,886 --> 00:03:50,508
Without that, another thread can see a non-null PriceTable whose map is still null, or half-built.

54
00:03:50,508 --> 00:03:55,778
Making the field final fixes it, and fixes it however the object is published.

55
00:03:56,228 --> 00:04:09,373
The four safe publication idioms: a static initialiser, a volatile or atomic reference, a final field set in the constructor, or a field always read and written under the same lock.

56
00:04:09,373 --> 00:04:13,295
Plus any handoff through java dot util dot concurrent.

57
00:04:14,195 --> 00:04:17,932
Double-checked locking is correct only with volatile.

58
00:04:17,932 --> 00:04:26,394
Without it, another thread can see the reference before the constructor's writes, a non-null engine with default fields.

59
00:04:26,394 --> 00:04:30,272
But usually there's a simpler answer: the holder idiom.

60
00:04:30,272 --> 00:04:34,644
Class initialisation is lazy and thread-safe by JVM guarantee.

61
00:04:34,644 --> 00:04:36,125
No volatile, no lock.

62
00:04:36,575 --> 00:04:39,529
And never let this escape the constructor.

63
00:04:39,529 --> 00:04:47,477
Registering yourself with an event bus inside the constructor means events can arrive before tracked is assigned.

64
00:04:47,477 --> 00:04:50,291
Final-field guarantees don't apply then.

65
00:04:50,291 --> 00:04:54,089
Construct first, then register, with a static factory.

66
00:04:54,089 --> 00:04:57,183
Java 21's this-escape lint warns about this.

67
00:04:58,083 --> 00:04:59,191
Three internals.

68
00:04:59,191 --> 00:05:00,713
Final field semantics.

69
00:05:00,713 --> 00:05:04,520
At the end of the constructor, final fields are frozen.

70
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:10,680
Any thread that gets the reference, by any means, sees them as the constructor left them.

71
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:16,494
That's why immutable objects need no synchronisation, as long as this didn't escape.

72
00:05:16,944 --> 00:05:17,616
Barriers.

73
00:05:17,616 --> 00:05:23,734
On x86, a volatile read is just a plain load, and a write is a locked instruction.

74
00:05:23,734 --> 00:05:25,749
On ARM, both need barriers.

75
00:05:25,749 --> 00:05:27,987
The memory model is identical.

76
00:05:27,987 --> 00:05:33,285
What differs is how much the hardware forgives missing synchronisation.

77
00:05:33,735 --> 00:05:36,202
And beware benign race arguments.

78
00:05:36,202 --> 00:05:39,342
String's cached hashCode races, correctly.

79
00:05:39,342 --> 00:05:46,071
But change the int to a long and it can tear, or read the field twice and get two answers.

80
00:05:46,071 --> 00:05:48,389
The JDK authors can justify it.

81
00:05:48,389 --> 00:05:50,482
Most application code can't.

82
00:05:51,382 --> 00:05:52,682
Production rules.

83
00:05:52,682 --> 00:05:56,888
Remove every data race, and then you can reason simply.

84
00:05:56,888 --> 00:06:01,935
Hand objects between threads through java dot util dot concurrent.

85
00:06:01,935 --> 00:06:04,764
Use final fields for anything shared.

86
00:06:04,764 --> 00:06:07,899
Use the holder idiom for lazy singletons.

87
00:06:07,899 --> 00:06:10,193
Construct first, then publish.

88
00:06:10,193 --> 00:06:13,099
And if you deploy to ARM, test on ARM.

89
00:06:13,549 --> 00:06:17,258
Never write double-checked locking without volatile.

90
00:06:17,258 --> 00:06:19,825
Never let this escape a constructor.

91
00:06:19,825 --> 00:06:22,392
Never publish through a plain field.

92
00:06:22,392 --> 00:06:24,888
Never trust works on my x86 laptop.

93
00:06:24,888 --> 00:06:28,739
And never argue a race is benign without a real proof.

94
00:06:29,639 --> 00:06:30,270
To recap.

95
00:06:30,270 --> 00:06:34,972
The memory model is a contract about which values reads may return.

96
00:06:34,972 --> 00:06:37,780
Happens-before chains a write to a read.

97
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:41,991
Remove every data race and the program behaves sequentially.

98
00:06:41,991 --> 00:06:45,500
One volatile write publishes everything before it.

99
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:49,500
And final fields, with no this-escape, make sharing safe.

100
00:06:49,950 --> 00:06:53,293
Next is chapter twenty-seven: locks and atomics.

101
00:06:53,293 --> 00:06:54,686
Thanks for watching.
